"""
SimpleCAD — PyQt5 + OpenGL
Single 3D view: plane selection switches to ortho top-view for sketching.
Constraint types: Fixed, Length, Diameter / Radius, Angle, Perpendicular, Parallel, Collinear, Equal,
Symmetric, Tangential, Coincident, Point-on-Line, Horizontal, Vertical.
Object tree: sketch01 → extrusion01 → sketch02 (on a face) → extrusion02 … (left dock);
imported models appear as independent root entries. The Properties panel shows object
information and exposes imported-model position and rotation fields.
Units: millimetres.

Menu: File (New, Open, Recent Files, Import, Save, Save As, Export STEP/STL or Technical Drawing, Exit) ·
Edit (incl. Measure and the configurable Settings) · View · Help.
Measure tool (toolbar "Measure" / Edit → Measure): click edges and corners of the solids (any
number); lengths, the relations between edges (connected / same face / parallel / perpendicular
with 3-D and 2-D XY values) and, for corners, the 3-D distance, ΔX / ΔY / ΔZ and angles are shown
in the status bar and in the Measurement panel below the object explorer. Measured edges and
corners are highlighted in the inverted colour of their body. RMB on a measured
item removes it, RMB elsewhere (or Esc) clears the measurement.
Toolbars: 1st row Undo / Redo; 2nd row New Sketch / Surface / Extrude / Ring Extrusion /
Angle Extrusion / Bridge / Chamfer / Fillet / Gear / Thread / Measurement, replaced in sketch mode by the
grouped sketch tools: Mode (Select / Draw / Constrain / Scissor / Delete), Draw tools (Line,
Rect, Circle, Ellipse, Polygon, Arc, Point, SVG import, Support), Constraints and Grid / snap. Select
is the default sketch mode. The right "Sketch Status"
panel shows name, plane, the constraint list, open issues / DOF and Accept / Cancel.
The 3-D axes are labelled x, y, z.
Projects are saved as .obj files (JSON content: every object of the explorer incl. sketch
geometry and constraints; solids are rebuilt on load). Import supports STEP (CadQuery/OCP)
and STL files; export writes STEP / STL meshes.

Workflow:
  1. Surface creates a displayed work plane relative to the origin, another Surface or a solid
     face. Planar faces align directly; curved faces create a tangent plane. Detected spheres can
     be positioned by azimuth/elevation and cylinders by angle/axial position. Other curved faces
     use an automatic tangent at their highest point. Set local XYZ offsets, local XYZ rotations
     and display size. Select a Surface and click New Sketch (or click it while picking a sketch
     plane) to sketch on it.
     While sketching on an attached Surface, its visible supporting solid is shown as a shaded
     silhouette and feature edges behind the sketch, including attached extrusions. The Surface
     rectangle does not limit this underlay. This visual underlay is not editable sketch geometry.
     Sketch Explorer shows parallel sketches grouped by signed distance along the active sketch's
     normal. Toggle each level's visibility; the current sketch cannot be hidden. Same-level
     sketches remain yellow and read-only. The nearest below level stays violet; deeper levels
     use darker violet shades, and above levels use darkening red shades.
     Enable Reference on a below level to highlight its fixed geometry while drawing, snap to its
     points, and select it in Select or Constrain mode. The direct supporting sketch is enabled
     automatically.
     Drawing attached to a reference creates a removable Coincident, Point-on-Line or
     Point-on-Curve constraint instead of merging the new geometry into the fixed reference.
     Hiding a level or disabling its reference checkbox does not remove existing constraints.
     Collapse the Sketch Explorer using its title-bar arrow, or reopen it from the View menu.
     Older face sketches may contain unidentifiable reference copies; those remain face references.
     Hiding a Surface hides only its display, not the sketches or extrusions built on it.
     Hiding a supporting solid still hides its dependent objects.
     Alternatively, New Sketch → click a plane (XY/XZ/YZ) or a flat face of an extrusion → 2-D sketch
     overlay appears. Esc / RMB cancels plane picking.
  2. Draw lines, rectangles, circles, ellipses (centre + two perpendicular diameters),
      regular polygons (centre + radius), arcs
      (centre, start, end), points or editable Text. A placement click opens the text editor;
      choose the font, size, style and alignment before confirming. Text is rigid: in Select
      mode, drag a label to move it, drag a bounding-box corner to resize it proportionally,
      or double-click it to edit. Its virtual box and nine constraint points remain visible.
    Import SVG paths and shapes or DXF geometry into the active sketch; convert complex SVG text layouts to paths first.
     Points snap to line crossings and onto lines (incl. support geometry), never to points.
     With "Support geometry" enabled the same tools create blue dashed construction
     geometry: it can carry constraints (optional) but is never part of the extruded profile.
     Support geometry always shows its length / diameter / radius as blue info labels.
     Scissor (trim): click a part of a line / circle / arc → it is cut away between the
     neighbouring crossings / points (a circle becomes an arc), e.g. to build rounded shapes.
     RMB on geometry: context menu — switch to support / normal geometry, Cut, Copy, Paste,
     Delete, Cancel (Ctrl+X / Ctrl+C / Ctrl+V also work; paste lands at the cursor).
     Select geometry, then Mirror Horizontal or Mirror Vertical and click a matching sketch line
     or sketch axis. The copies remain constrained to their originals and follow changes to the
     axis. Text cannot be mirrored. Endpoints on the axis remain separate, so they can move away.
     On a face, the face outline is shown as fixed (violet) reference geometry; it cannot
     be deleted, cut or trimmed. In Constrain mode it can serve as a fixed reference;
     lines and coplanar circles from the extrusion's source sketch are also projected as fixed
     references. Equal supports line length and circle diameter; Parallel and Symmetric use lines.
     overlapping drawn geometry takes priority when picking.
     New face sketches use a consistent surface-normal view: world +Z is up on side faces,
     world +Y on horizontal faces, independent of the 3-D camera. A bottom-left indicator
     shows the world axes projected onto the surface and stays visible when panning.
    3. Add constraints (Length, Diameter / Radius, Angle, Fix, Perpendicular, Parallel,
        Collinear, Equal, Symmetric, Tangential, Point→Point / Line, Point on Line). Length
        measures between entities; Diameter / Radius sets a circle dimension. Angle is a separate
        two-line constraint. Tangential accepts a line and circle/arc,
      or two circles/arcs. Point→Point detects its target: point → coincident, line → point on
     line, circle/arc → point on curve, origin → fixed at (0,0). They are
     solved numerically: dimensions drive the geometry, dragging only moves geometry within
     its remaining freedom, and redundant / conflicting constraints are rejected.
    In support-geometry mode the Length and Angle tools only measure (information, in brackets).
     Point–point distances can be direct, horizontal (ΔX) or vertical (ΔY); a horizontal /
     vertical distance of 0 aligns two points exactly.
    DOF 0 = profile fully constrained (green); Accept needs a closed profile. Text outlines
    form closed profiles too and can be extruded.
     The constraint list shows every constraint (select = highlight, remove, edit value).
     In Select mode dimensions can be dragged to a better position (double-click = edit).
  4. Accept sketch → returns to 3-D view; sketch appears in Objects tree.
     Esc with nothing pending cancels the sketch (asks if it has geometry). RMB never closes
     the sketch: it deselects or aborts the current drawing step / constraint pick / tool.
     Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y undo / redo every sketch step (drawing clicks, constraints, drags, …).
  5. Select sketch in tree → Extrude → direction (one direction / opposite / symmetric),
     extent (distance, through all touching bodies [cuts], or to the next surface: the next
     extrusion, sketch region or coordinate plane in line with the extrusion, straight only),
     "Remove material" → OK. Opacity is set per object in the Properties panel (no edit mode).
     A click on a body in the 3-D view selects that surface only (the body as a whole is
     selected in the object explorer); with "To next surface" the clicked face becomes the target.
    Extrusion appears under its parent sketch in the Objects tree; a valid extrusion preview
    hides its source sketch in 3D. Cancelling restores the previous visibility (show or hide
    the accepted extrusion's source sketch with the tree checkbox).
     Bridge connects solid faces: click Bridge, then click faces on the two bodies. For two
     extrusions, if those faces do not oppose each other, Bridge searches for a suitable pair.
     A curved CAD face (such as a cylinder) can be the target even when it has multiple boundary
     loops; the first face's boundary is projected onto it. The selected solids and bridge are
     fused into one new solid under the first selected solid in the Objects tree; the other
     source remains linked to the Bridge result. Original solids are retained but hidden.
     A "remove material"
     extrusion is drawn as a translucent red tool body and is subtracted (BSP-tree CSG) from
     every body created before it that it passes through; exports contain the cut bodies.
  6. File → Import: STEP / STL models centered at the origin; imported models can be moved
     and rotated around X/Y/Z with the XYZ fields in the Objects properties panel. STEP models
     also have a Fixed position/orientation checkbox. New Sketch can use a planar STEP face;
     dependent sketches automatically fix the model until the last dependency is deleted (or a
     new sketch is cancelled). A manually fixed model stays fixed until its checkbox is cleared.
    File → Export writes the selected solid (or all visible solids) as STEP or STL.
    Choose Technical Drawing to open its editor, then select the extrusion in the Views tab.
    Place the top, front, left, right, isometric, and optional section views manually on the A2 sheet
    (DIN EN ISO 5457 frame, grid and title block; first-angle projection per DIN ISO 128-30 by default:
    top view below the front view, left view to its right; the third-angle method can be chosen on the
    Sheet tab). Scales are the preferred ones of ISO 5455, dimension text is unit-less (mm) and follows
    DIN 406-11; the title block carries the general tolerances (ISO 2768).
    Rotate the model in XYZ quarter-turns, position cuts by clicking a projection, and place
    entered dimensions between two picked points. The drawing is saved as a child of the extrusion.
    Select the drawing and choose Technical Drawing again to export it as PDF or SVG.
    Assembly (toolbar button right of Technical Drawing, also in the File / Edit menus) opens a
    window of its own with a 3-D view, a component explorer and an Alignments panel. Add parts takes
    parent solids of the project (everything built on them comes along); Import adds STEP / STL /
    OBJ files (a SimpleCAD project .obj contributes each of its parent solids; its child extrusions
    are included in the component but not listed separately in the Assembly explorer). Parts are
    snapshots that cannot be edited there, only placed: the selected part carries yellow arrows
    (move along X / Y / Z) and green rings (rotate about X / Y / Z; rotation snaps every 15°,
    Ctrl = free; Shift snaps the move to whole mm), or use the XYZ
    fields. Align (click a surface, a corner, an edge midpoint or a hole rim = centre on the part
    to move, then the same on another part; Alt + click on a surface = object centre) creates a
    constraint listed in the
    Alignments panel: two surfaces are kept parallel and in one plane, points coincide. It follows
    when the target part moves, Apply restores it, unticking releases it.
    A part whose source changed is marked (outdated); Update Selected / Update All re-read the
    sources and keep the placement. Save / Open handle .asby files and Export writes the complete
    assembly as STEP or STL. The assembly is also saved in the project file (key "assembly"),
    outside the undo history.
  7. Explorer context menu / Edit menu: Edit (sketch → drawing mode, extrusion → extrude
     sidebar; dependent objects are rebuilt), Rename (F2), Delete (Del, asks Yes/No and also
     removes dependent objects).
  8. Undo / redo (Edit menu, toolbar, Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y / Ctrl+Shift+Z): inside a sketch every
     sketch step; in the 3-D view every project change (sketch accepted, extrusion, delete,
     rename, visibility, colour, edits). Snapshot based (JSON of the sketch / project).
    9. Errors: uncaught exceptions are written to simplecad_error.txt (next to the script, or
     the temp folder) instead of ending the program. Edit → Settings → Debug switches the
     simplecad_debug.txt output on / off.

Key implementation notes:
  - state["objects"] is a flat list of entry dicts {type, name, parent, visible, ...}.
    Extrusion "parent" = its sketch entry; sketch "parent" = extrusion it was drawn on (or None).
    Imported meshes are root entries, centered locally with editable XYZ position and rotation.
    STEP face frames and outlines are saved with the mesh; child sketches lock its position.
  - Visibility: tree checkbox = entry["visible"]; each object's visibility is independent.
    Only explicit "with children" / "children" actions change descendant visibility.
  - QTreeWidgetItem.setData stores id(entry) (int), NOT the dict itself.
    PyQt5 copies dicts through QVariant, breaking identity checks.
    _on_tree_click looks up the real entry via next(o for o in objects if id(o)==eid).
  - Sketch.frame = (origin, u, v, w): sketch x/y axes in 3-D and extrusion direction.
  - Solid entries carry "faces" (top/bottom/side per edge) used for per-face hover
    highlighting, planar-face sketches, and datum surfaces tangent to curved faces.
    Generated chamfer/fillet faces retain their feature entry's runtime identity so selection
    highlights that individual operation, never all operations of the same type.
  - SketchSystem: unknowns = vertex x/y + circle radii; DOF = unknowns − rank(Jacobian).
  - circ.radius always stores the true radius. CDimension.dim_mode ("diameter"/"radius")
    selects what the value means; extrusion reads circ.radius directly.
  - Arcs are CCW from v_start to v_end; radius = |center→v_start|, the solver keeps v_end on it.
  - Dimensions are drawn in technical-drawing style (extension lines, arrows, aligned text).
  - 3-D view: LMB drag rotates, MMB drag pans, wheel zooms; the hovered axis / plane /
    solid face is highlighted and a LMB click (without drag) selects the hovered object.
    - Visible sketch geometry is thin green in 3-D. Selecting an extrusion highlights all connected
        non-cut extrusions through sketch parent/child links; related sketches are not highlighted.
  - RMB in sketch mode: deselects, else aborts the current action (never closes the sketch).
  - Snapping: the cursor snaps to an existing point within VERTEX_SNAP_PX screen pixels,
    otherwise to the snap grid; vertices only merge when exactly coincident (MERGE_TOL).
  - Point→Point: the first point moves onto the second (which is held still if possible),
    so unconstrained geometry stretches; the origin can also be the target.
  - RMB in 3-D mode deselects; RMB in the tree opens Edit / Rename / Delete / visibility.
  - _rebuild_tree must be called after every change to state["objects"].

Python 3.10, Windows. Packages: PyQt5 5.15, numpy 2.2, PyOpenGL 3.1. DXF sketch import uses ezdxf.
STEP import/export also uses CadQuery/OCP; STL import has no extra dependency. UI styling uses qt-modern-style 2.0.1.
"""
